---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatMarkdownTable from '@/components/tools/data/DevFormatMarkdownTable.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="csv-to-markdown-table">
  <DevFormatMarkdownTable client:load mode="csv-to-md" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Why a naive comma split is not enough</h2>
    <p>
      Many quick CSV to Markdown converters split each line on commas. That fails when a field contains
      a comma, which happens quickly in real data: names (&ldquo;Doe, Jane&rdquo;), addresses, currency
      in some locales, and free-text columns. RFC 4180 defines the escape (wrap the field in double
      quotes, and double each quote inside it), and this page implements it.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>CSV field</th><th>Correct value</th><th>What a naive split produces</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>"Doe, Jane"</code></td><td>Doe, Jane</td><td>Two columns</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>"She said ""hi"""</code></td><td>She said "hi"</td><td>Stray quotes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A quoted field containing a newline</td><td>One multi-line value</td><td>Two broken rows</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>,,</code></td><td>Two empty fields</td><td>Usually collapsed to one</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Delimiter detection</h2>
    <p>
      The parser checks the first rows for the separator that gives a consistent column count, and it
      recognises comma, tab, semicolon and pipe. This matters:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Data copied from a spreadsheet is tab-separated, not comma-separated.</li>
      <li>Excel in a locale where the comma is the decimal separator exports semicolon-separated files and still calls them CSV.</li>
      <li>Log and database exports often use a pipe.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The detected delimiter shows next to the input, so you can see a wrong guess before you copy.</p>

    <h2>Getting the header right</h2>
    <p>
      A GFM table always shows its first row as a header; the format has no headerless table. If your
      CSV starts with data, turn off <strong>first row is a header</strong> in the output options. The
      tool then generates a <code>Column 1</code>, <code>Column 2</code> header and keeps all your rows.
      If your CSV has two header rows, merge them in the grid first.
    </p>

    <h2>Alignment for numeric columns</h2>
    <p>
      Right-aligned numbers make a data table much easier to read: the digits line up by place value,
      and a reader can compare sizes at a glance. Click the alignment button of a column to cycle left,
      centre and right. This writes the colon markers into the separator row. Use left for text, right
      for numbers and dates, and centre seldom.
    </p>

    <h2>Edit before you export</h2>
    <p>
      The parsed CSV goes into an editable grid before it becomes Markdown. You can delete columns you do
      not need, fix a typo, remove an empty last row, and set alignment before any output is generated.
      This is usually faster than cleaning the CSV first, and much faster than fixing the Markdown
      later.
    </p>

    <h2>Large files</h2>
    <p>
      There is no row limit and no upload, so the limit is the number of input fields your browser can
      render. A few thousand rows is fine; a hundred thousand is not, and for that size a script is
      better than a web page. For mid-sized files, paste the header and a sample, get the shape right,
      then convert the full file with <a href="/csv-to-json">CSV to JSON</a> or a spreadsheet.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
